#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "a1068af3-0424-51ae-8567-7e1503209d2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5493" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-5x_{2} + 8x_{3} = 0; 3x_{3} = 0; 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_2=0$.\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124252 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d945f26-273d-5648-bdee-9092ea61110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a Two-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "e82a8038-a2fa-547d-b3ec-32e8f0e4babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5494" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-10x_{1} + 8x_{2} - 6x_{3} = 0; - 15x_{1} + 12x_{2} - 9x_{3} = 0; - 5x_{1} + 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 4 5x_2-\\dfrac35x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}x_{2} - \\frac{3}{5}x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124253 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"}] :question/id #uuid "7f15ec22-8a8e-5547-9aa1-7ffa8b1555a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5495" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{0}$ and $V_{-2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda=0$ and $\\lambda=-2$ of $A$, respectively?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) = 0 \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 6\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\\\\n0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 3 - λ &= 0 \\\\\n(-2 - λ) \\mid 1 - λ\\begin{vmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}- 3 - λ &= 0 \\\\\n&= (-2 - λ)((1 - λ)(-3 - λ) + 3) = 0 \\\\\n&= - (λ + 2)(λ^{2} + 2λ) = 0 \\\\\n&= λ(λ + 2)^{2} = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda_1=0$ (simple root) and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n(double root).\n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda=0$. Since $V_{0}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain\n$\\textrm{dim}(V_{0})=1$.\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda=-2$, which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of $V_{-2}$, we row-reduce the matrix $(A - (-2) I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$:\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{1}{3})R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -2$. Therefore,\n$\\textrm{dim}(V_{-2})=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124254 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e7473be-4d68-5d31-817f-adb7cf288e97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "483a0b64-adfa-50b7-a738-2cdc135b1163" :question/math-academy-id "e-5496" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2$ shown above.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$, what is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{3x_{1} - 6x_{2} + 5x_{3} = 0; 6x_{1} - 12x_{2} + 10x_{3} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=2x_2-\\dfrac{5}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x_{2} - \\frac{5}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now deduce the eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2\\mathbin$:\n\n- Since $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have that $x_2=1$ and $x_3=0$. As a result, $a=2(1)-\\dfrac 53(0)=2$.\n- Since $\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have that $x_2=0$ and $x_3=3$. As a result, $b=2(0)-\\dfrac53(3)=-5$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b=2+(-5)=-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e7473be-4d68-5d31-817f-adb7cf288e97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b75ffab5-ef96-522f-a173-1808438ed2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122573} {:answer/id #uuid "e80be7be-b1b9-54db-8c55-0cf7dac2fd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122574} {:answer/id #uuid "2042aaca-9d28-5bcb-8697-33a9c87d83dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122575} {:answer/id #uuid "230b3010-f671-57e0-aa73-803d67206726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122576} {:answer/id #uuid "44803fe4-6dc2-579f-a4f7-4eec8215b3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75ffab5-ef96-522f-a173-1808438ed2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122573} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfbb28a1-875e-5940-877e-7bcb1b748ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122572}] :question/id #uuid "3468c6de-6c90-537b-abad-213a5aa7babe" :question/math-academy-id "q-36760" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $v_{1}, v_{2}$ shown above.\nIf $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$, what is the value of $a + b$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c374705-95dd-559d-85bb-8a168ec46d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 1$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122669} {:answer/id #uuid "c474c32d-3213-581d-964d-966f7be66190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 0$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122670} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bda879-39d8-597b-a0eb-f55a665b98ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 2$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122671} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9d2958-2a26-5fe0-ab29-f612f0fca960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 2$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122672} {:answer/id #uuid "574c3d11-b9ed-5817-a335-66ad12d32e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 1$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574c3d11-b9ed-5817-a335-66ad12d32e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$dim(V_{-6}) = 1$ and $dim(V_{2}) = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122673} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d0e1f5-06b9-5e39-bfcb-3857f22fbdae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122668}] :question/id #uuid "b330ccf8-bb5f-5d71-ad55-322ed64e004b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36981" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{-6}$ and $V_{2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $λ =-6$ and $λ = 2$ of $A$, respectively?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d945f26-273d-5648-bdee-9092ea61110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a Two-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f667c6fc-a4e2-5bc8-8766-c3da49db478b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122675} {:answer/id #uuid "1b99de74-490e-5faf-bd8e-710d6fb554cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122676} {:answer/id #uuid "28da254a-9dcb-5253-b12a-73d131f1112a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122677} {:answer/id #uuid "488db4cd-b8a8-5539-b48f-82160259f315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122678} {:answer/id #uuid "5242ed92-f48a-566d-b6a2-5c66710166d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5242ed92-f48a-566d-b6a2-5c66710166d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122679} :answer-field/id #uuid "4545f41f-2114-5738-8fa6-fa3da2c4f410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122674}] :question/id #uuid "28660a25-cd5f-5bd7-9a7a-c2615cd1df35" :question/math-academy-id "q-36983" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5f5ac8-a32f-549d-bbd8-a58cd047f211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122999} {:answer/id #uuid "d67b047f-734f-5326-894f-f75b881d9747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123000} {:answer/id #uuid "fee07fd5-59fe-53d7-97de-007f5271df78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123001} {:answer/id #uuid "4877c303-7602-5616-a988-32e667c04592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123002} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8064e8-d1d2-5b8e-a107-97cd66fabe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5f5ac8-a32f-549d-bbd8-a58cd047f211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122999} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f759e8c-4ffa-5b96-bb8b-fea16b2e535d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122998}] :question/id #uuid "7bd51e17-cb8e-56d8-899b-9ac7a2d75c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-37581" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 5$?"}]}
